## Canonical body structure

```markdown
## The rule

<One-paragraph statement of what IS authorized.>

## Verbatim directive

<The principal's exact words when granting, with session context.>

## Motivation

<Why this authorization exists.  Usually because friction costs
outweighed quality-gate benefits for this class of action.>

## What this authorizes

<Concrete enumerated list — specific actions that are pre-approved.>

## What this does NOT authorize

<Explicit carve-outs — the boundary between this authorization and
per-instance approval.>

## The filter

<The quality gate that substitutes for per-instance approval.  Usually:
existing protocols + self-code-review + tests.>

## How to apply — decision procedure

<Step-by-step: when facing an action that might qualify, what to
check + in what order.>

## Risks

<Failure modes this authorization enables.  What should the principal
watch for?  What signals suggest the authorization should be tightened
or rescinded?>

## When to still ask

<Specific conditions that override the authorization and return to
per-instance approval.>

## Re-evaluation triggers

<When to proactively re-review the authorization.>

## History

<Timeline:
- YYYY-MM-DD: granted (session context; verbatim)
- YYYY-MM-DD: scope adjustment
- YYYY-MM-DD: status change
>

## See also

<Adjacent authorizations, governing protocols, governing preferences.>
```

## Lifecycle

Authorization memorials are **long-lived, status-tracked**:

- Created on `granted-on` date with status: active
- May transition to `suspended` (reversible; history entry required)
- May transition to `rescinded` (history entry; authorization effectively frozen; still kept as forensic record)
- Amended via in-place edits for scope clarifications (history entry required)
- **Never deleted** — the forensic trail of what was authorized when compounds across the project

## State-change-detection (captureability)

This class is mid-conversation-captureable.  Inject-triggers:
- *"you can stop asking"*
- *"I authorize..."*

When these signals fire, capture-first with verbatim directive.

## Pitfalls

- **Pre-authorizing yourself** — authorization memorials are created when the principal grants, not when the AI proposes.  If self-authorizing, stop and ask first
- **Broad scope** — narrow is better.  *"Authorized to refactor anything"* is too broad; *"authorized to improve algorithmic complexity when tooling is noticeably slow, preserving behavior and tests"* is scoped
- **Missing risks section** — an authorization without explicit failure modes is a blank check.  Always name what can go wrong
- **Paraphrasing the verbatim directive** — capture exact words.  Paraphrase elsewhere; verbatim is the epistemic anchor
- **Status drift** — if an authorization is no longer being exercised for months, consider whether it's still needed; mark suspended if so
- **Treating per-commit authorization as categorical** — per-commit authorization is load-bearing and should NEVER fold into a standing authorization

## Composition with adjacent classes

- **`feedback`** — where authorizations lived previously; promotions are common
- **`decision`** — adjacent type; decisions are code/architecture choices, authorizations are meta-decisions about collaboration
- **`log`** — every authorization grant or status change is logged; the log captures the session, the authorization captures the standing rule
- **`event`** — authorization grants are events; the timeline of grants forms the collaboration's history

